  "body": "We have a situation where we have an iframe that shows content that we *theoretically* control. For example, rendered HTML of user-generated markdown. Due to the fact that vulnerabilities are often discovered in markdown renderers, we prefer to show the rendered HTML on a separate origin to avoid XSS. So, something like `main-origin.com` loads an iframe with `main-origin-less-trusted.com`. There are instances where we generate our own page entirely (for example, if the user content is just an image), and thus we prefer to just show some information about the file and a download link. I would prefer to not have to add the huge indiscriminate \"allow-downloads\" flag to the iframe, but instead do something like `allow-download-from-origin=\"main-origin-downloads.com\"`, that way if we make a download link it works, but we don't have to worry about user generated content creating download links. I tried to see if there was some sort of CSP way of doing this (connect-src?), but that appears to not be the case.",
